      "string" : "Information Aggregation in Sequential\nMarkets: A Fixture\nAnonymous\nAbstract\nWe study how sequential traders aggregate private signals. Our\nidentification strategy exploits the staggered rollout of a disclosure\nrule. The effect is statistically significant but economically small.\n1 Introduction\nHerdinghasbeenstudiedextensivelysinceBanerjee[1992]andBikhchandani\net al. [1992]. Later work extends the framework to markets [Avery and Zem-\nsky, 1998, Smith and Sørensen, 2000]. As shown by Acemoglu et al. [2011],\nnetwork structure determines whether learning is efficient; see also [Smith\nand Sørensen, 2000, p. 12] and Kogan [2020], Lee [2021]. The coefficient in\nTable 2 is 0.91 with a confidence interval that crosses one, which is difficult to\nreconcile with the claim of a robust effect. We define the office-specific iden-\ntification threshold and afterwards verify the differentiability of the payoff\n1"
